Executive Pitch (100 Words)
MarketPulse is a smart market watchlist that surfaces only meaningful changes instead of overwhelming users with noise. Rather than showing every price tick, domain-driven engines detect truly meaningful movements: stocks outperforming peers, volume anomalies, correlation shifts. Users set an attention budget (top 3, 5, 10, or all) and see only actionable insights ranked by importance. Built end-to-end with React, Node.js, and PostgreSQL—from design through deployment—the system handles stale data gracefully, scales horizontally, and is thoroughly tested. Our insight: meaningful change is relative, not absolute. What matters is context.

Problem Statement
Stock watchlists are noisy. Users get overwhelmed by daily price ticks, percentage changes, and information overload. They miss opportunities because meaningful movements are buried in noise.

The experience breaks down at the decision point: A user adds NVIDIA to their watchlist, checks back tomorrow, and sees "+2%". Is that significant? They don't know without checking:

How did the sector perform? (+0.5% = NVDA outperformed)
Is there unusual volume? (Today: 42M vs. 30M average)
Are other holdings moving the same direction? (Correlation jumped)
These aren't obvious questions. Answering them requires context that watchlist apps don't provide.

The core insight: Users don't want more data. They want better data.

Solution: MarketPulse
MarketPulse detects meaningful changes using domain-driven engines that analyze:

1. Price Movement with Peer Context
Not: "Stock moved 2%" Yes: "Stock moved 5% while sector moved 1% (outperforming by 4 percentage points)"

2. Volume Anomalies
Detects when trading volume spikes relative to 20-day average, indicating unusual activity.

3. Correlation Shifts
When holdings start moving together, individual stock picks matter less. The system detects this.

4. Attention Ranking
Instead of "show me everything," users choose: "Show me top 3" or "I want all 20." Respects attention as finite.

Result: Users see 3 truly important changes instead of scrolling 20 trivial ticks.

